  "query_preview": "/Wednesday through Saturday/...\nAn unusually early season sudden stratospheric warming (SSW) event \nwill be the primary influence towards hemispheric pattern \namplification this week featuring ridge retrogression poleward and \ndeep negative height anomalies descending into the NE Conus and East \nPacific. During the initial stages of this transition, strong \nsubsident high pressure with nearly steady midtropospheric heights \nwill be situated directly over Arizona yielding a high confidence \nforecast of a very tranquil, near persistence temperatures a couple \ndegrees above normal. Late in the week, there is growing ensemble \nagreement that shortwave energy will detach from the larger East \nPacific trough and progress towards the SW Conus/northern Mexico. \nHowever, significant spread persists regarding the timing of \nincoming negative height anomalies and jet energy that would \ninfluence regional weather. Given the evolution of this highly \namplified and semi-blocked pattern, would generally prefer a \nsolution towards the slower end of the ensemble envelope bringing \nheightened rainfall chances into the CWA Saturday/Sunday. \nRegardless, run-to-run variations are still likely the next several \ndays with respect to details in timing and location of the best \nforcing resulting in limited forecast confidence.",
